+## `domain-availability` (2026-08-01): a request whose literal core is technically unreachable from this sandbox
+"checks domain available... pull in whatever public info... check across a
+wide variety of tlds" — the first request from this requester that isn't a
+game/diagram/simulation but a lookup utility, and the first where the literal
+ask (live WHOIS/registry availability) is flatly impossible: `connect-src` in
+`lab/www/worker.js` only allows Bluesky/auth hosts, so no client-side fetch
+can reach any domain registry. No back-and-forth yet to confirm the read, but
+the resolution taken — build every part that *is* real (syntax validation,
+curated TLD facts, eligibility rules) and hand off the actual live check to a
+one-click outbound link (`<a href>` navigation isn't governed by
+`connect-src`, only fetch/XHR/WebSocket are) rather than faking a yes/no — is
+the same pattern as `chladni-sim`'s language-substitution call: say the
+constraint plainly (banner on the page itself, not just BRIEF.md), build the
+strongest honest equivalent, don't silently deliver a fake version of what
+was asked. Durable: when a request's literal core needs a network host
+outside `connect-src`, check whether the *unreachable* part can be
+delegated to a plain outbound link before concluding the request can't be
+served at all — CSP blocks fetch, not navigation.
